detail.abstract:
Problem reframing has long been recognized as an important aspect of decision-making and innovation. However, prior research often treats reframing as a one-time cognitive activity, without clearly explaining where and how it operates within decision processes. At the same time, emerging AI-enabled systems suggest that problem definitions are increasingly fluid and continuously reshaped.&nbsp;
<div>
 This paper develops a structural account of problem reframing by locating it within a five-dimensional decision architecture consisting of Problem, Data, Learning, Generation, and Governance. Rather than viewing reframing as an isolated event, the study shows that it can occur at different stages of decision processes and be triggered by distinct sources.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Four types of reframing are identified: context-driven, behavior-driven, signal-driven, and AI-driven. While the first three are externally triggered, AI-driven reframing emerges from the interaction between learning and generation, enabling the system to continuously produce alternative problem formulations.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This shift suggests that decision-making is no longer only about selecting optimal solutions under fixed problems, but increasingly about navigating and generating alternative problem definitions. The findings provide a structural explanation for recent empirical observations of AI participation in problem formation, including evidence from semiconductor manufacturing, engineering design, and logistics systems (Huang &amp; Lin, 2026). They also point to new implications for governance and organizational design in human-AI collaborative environments, particularly as AI-enabled decision systems and emerging AI agent environments become more widespread.
</div>

